body:
I'm sorry! I thought I had covered everything, but I realized today that it seems wrong to hardcode `2.0` here. Shouldn't this instead be something like:

```suggestion
float DisplayServerVisionOS::_screen_potential_edr_headroom() const {
	return [UIScreen mainScreen].potentialEDRHeadroom;
}

float DisplayServerVisionOS::_screen_current_edr_headroom() const {
	return [UIScreen mainScreen].currentEDRHeadroom;
}
```

When I watch the linked video, I understand this to be simply informational about the current state of apple vision pro devices. I do not get the feeling from this video that the developer should ignore the `potentialEDRHeadroom` and `currentEDRHeadroom` screen values and always force them to be `2.0`.

My concern with hardcoding these is that if Apple comes out with a new visionOS headset, it may have different `currentEDRHeadroom` or `potentialEDRHeadroom`. In a worst-case, these could be lower than `2.0`, resulting in hard-clipped colours. In a less bad case, these could be greater than `2.0`, resulting in not being able to make use of the hardware's capabilities.

Thoughts?

